Also, since the smaller tetrahedron is similar to the larger tetrahedron, the ratio of the height of the smaller tetrahedron to the height of the larger tetrahedron is equal to the ratio of the sides of the smaller tetrahedron to the sides of the larger tetrahedron. Therefore, the height of the smaller tetrahedron is $\frac{1}{3}$ of the height of the larger tetrahedron. 

The ratio of the volumes of two similar solids is equal to the cube of the ratio of their corresponding sides. Therefore, the ratio of the volume of the smaller tetrahedron to the volume of the larger tetrahedron is equal to the cube of the ratio of the height of the smaller tetrahedron to the height of the larger tetrahedron. Since the height of the smaller tetrahedron is $\frac{1}{3}$ of the height of the larger tetrahedron, the ratio of the volumes is $\left(\frac{1}{3}\right)^3=\frac{1}{27}$.

