Two circles have the same center O. Point X is the midpoint of segment OP. What is the ratio of the area of the circle with radius OX to the area of the circle with radius OP? Express your answer as a common fraction.

[asy]
import graph;
draw(Circle((0,0),20));
draw(Circle((0,0),12));
dot((0,0));
dot((20,0));
dot((12,0));
draw((0,0)--(20,0));
label("$O$",(0,0),SE);
label("$P$",(20,0),E);
label("$X$",(12,0),SE);
[/asy]

solution_steps:
The area of a circle is proportional to the square of its radius.
Since OP is twice the length of OX, the area of the circle with radius OX is $(\frac{1}{2})^2=\frac{1}{4}$ that of the circle with radius OP.
Therefore, the ratio of the area of the circle with radius OX to the area of the circle with radius OP is $\boxed{\frac{1}{4}}$.The answer is: \frac{1}{4}

